arm64-darwin  clang emits ___cxa_free_exception and cxx1 does not. It is the cleanup for a copy constructor that throws while the exception object is being initialised: the storage __cxa_allocate_exception handed back has an owner for the length of that copy and nobody afterwards, so clang guards the copy with a landing pad that frees it. cxx1 does not, and leaks the allocation on that path. This is the open item docs/HANDOVER records as `__cxa_free_exception`, and it became *visible* here rather than new: until the exception object was copy-constructed there was no copy that could throw, so no case named the symbol. Nothing else differs, and the leak needs a throwing copy constructor to reach - which nothing in this tree has, cxx1 having no bad_alloc to raise.
x86_64-linux  the same one symbol, spelled __cxa_free_exception.
